## Current disposition — 2026-10-06

The bounded Sites artifact, Drizzle migration and normal-owner bootstrap compatibility repair is implemented through merged #1348 (`6cd279954199e640bb4b913f118207c378dfb1fb`). Close the source repair. Actual private Sites rollout remains deferred in #1354; current hosted file-backed continuation and schema-3 adoption remain with #1372. A separate Cloudflare result is not Sites qualification.

This user-authorized reconciliation is tracked in #1209. Earlier scopes and evidence below are preserved verbatim as history; their then-current Draft/open wording is superseded only by this disposition. No source/runtime, verification, provider, migration, deployment or merge operation is performed by closing this issue.

---

## Preserved earlier task and evidence record

## Outcome and scope

Parent hosted acceptance: #1345. Reviewed implementation: merged PR #1346.

A real Sites-capable deployment attempt on 2026-09-27 built the reviewed app successfully but did **not** create or deploy a Site. It demonstrated a concrete compatibility gap between the repository's custom hosting artifact and the current Sites packaging/database path:

- Current merged main observed by that attempt: `a2fb77b5d16684814462e0c5dd44ad1a9d2b1f46`, tree `2c2a74778652a7b58bb7fe2c01bdf2a1f57dbd0d`.
- `npm run web:build` succeeded. Built legacy worker SHA-256: `154a02ff04aa5b8e79939d53ece3f111d70631b3b54383e72197c19006f2c7ef`.
- The current build emits `dist/web-planning/worker.mjs` plus a repository-defined `artifact.json`; the actual Sites packaging check required a standard server artifact at `dist/server/index.js`.
- The observed Sites D1 path uses Drizzle migrations/journal metadata; the repository currently stages only `migrations/0001.sql`, with no established Sites migration admission.
- The available hosted D1 management surface did not expose an arbitrary write path to insert the required initial `web_planning_workspace` row.
- The repository's `artifact.json` compatibility date/flags were not observed as a supported Sites configuration input.
- No Site/D1/version/URL/secret/remote config was created; existing Sites and data were unchanged.

This is not evidence that the application server logic failed. It is a demonstrated hosting-adapter/build/bootstrap gap. Fix the smallest supported compatibility layer and return one Draft PR. Do **not** deploy during this issue; #1345 remains the hosted acceptance owner after review/merge.

## Current platform evidence to inspect first

Recheck current primary sources and the actual available Sites tool schema before choosing exact mechanisms.

Current official references include:
- https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/sites
- https://github.com/openai/sites
- https://github.com/openai/sites/tree/main/packages/create-sites
- https://github.com/openai/sites/tree/main/packages/sites-vite-plugin

At issue creation, OpenAI's current public tooling states:
- `@openai/create-sites` provides the current Sites starter; its D1 add-on uses Drizzle and a `DB` binding.
- `@openai/sites-vite-plugin` copies required `.openai/hosting.json` to `dist/.openai/hosting.json` and optional `drizzle/**` to `dist/.openai/drizzle/**`.
- The current official starter uses the Cloudflare/Vite hosting toolchain and `nodejs_compat`; do not treat the repository's custom `artifact.json` as a platform contract.
- Separate OpenAI starter/runtime material expects a Sites deployment server artifact at `dist/server/index.js`.

These observations can change; pin/report exact upstream versions/commits actually used in the PR. Do not blindly migrate the application to Vinext/React merely because the starter uses it. Preserve the existing Web Planning product and Worker semantics unless the actual runtime proves a narrower adapter impossible.

## Required outcome

Produce a Sites-compatible deployment artifact from the **same reviewed Web Planning behavior**, while preserving local D1 correctness, native Augnes isolation and fail-closed identity semantics.

### 1. Standard Sites artifact

Replace or supersede the custom deployment staging so the supported build emits the exact current Sites-required shape.

At minimum, qualify the actual control-plane requirement for:
- `dist/server/index.js` as the Worker server entry;
- `dist/.openai/hosting.json`;
- any required `dist/client` content (do not invent a dummy client merely because another starter has one);
- the supported runtime configuration path for `nodejs_compat` and any compatibility date actually required;
- D1 binding `DB`.

Prefer the current official OpenAI Sites/Vite/Cloudflare build mechanism when it can wrap the existing raw Worker without product rewrite. If a smaller staging adapter is actually supported, prove it with the same packager/contract used by Sites. Do not keep `artifact.json` as authoritative unless the current platform explicitly consumes it.

Keep the production bundle free of local synthetic login, native runtime/database, test harnesses, secrets and real user material.

### 2. Drizzle/Sites migration compatibility

Create one authoritative hosted-schema source compatible with the current Sites Drizzle path.

Preserve all current schema behavior:
- schema version gate;
- one workspace mapping;
- immutable revision chain and unique revision/request keys;
- revision 1..32 bound;
- JSON-valid envelope;
- erased-ID guard;
- composite workspace/project foreign-key scope.

Use current official Sites-compatible Drizzle versions/tooling unless current source inspection justifies another supported version. Generated migration/journal material must be deterministic and staged where Sites actually consumes it.

Avoid two silently diverging schema sources. Either make the Drizzle migration the local D1 test source as well, or retain the old SQL only with an explicit parity/consumer reason and a failing drift check.

The schema-version row may be seeded deterministically by migration. No real owner identity, secret or project-specific private value belongs in committed migrations.

### 3. Initial owner/workspace mapping without an unsafe setup API

First inspect whether the actual Sites deployment/database tools provide a supported, reviewable initialization/seed write for the one mapping row. If they do, use that path and document/test it.

If they do **not**, this issue authorizes the smallest application bootstrap that preserves the original authority intent:
- no public/general setup endpoint;
- no “first visitor becomes owner” behavior;
- the owner is still deliberately preconfigured in server environment;
- initialization is possible only after the normal exact-origin checks, trusted Sites ingress mode, authenticated principal == configured owner, valid configured workspace/project/author IDs, compatible empty schema and no existing mapping;
- use a race-safe conditional insert, then reread and require the exact expected row;
- an existing mismatched row always refuses; it is never rebound or repaired automatically;
- missing/untrusted identity, unset ingress mode or direct-backend-style access cannot initialize;
- retry is idempotent and cannot create a second mapping.

If another current platform-native method is simpler and equally preserves deliberate ownership, use it instead and explain why.

Do not put the owner's plaintext email or other private identity in migration/source/build artifacts. Do not expose REQUEST_SECRET or create a credential-bearing initialization URL.

### 4. Compatibility and security checks

Keep existing Web Planning behavior unchanged outside the demonstrated hosting/bootstrap responsibility.

Add discriminating tests for:
- exact Sites package paths and absence of the obsolete custom-only contract;
- standard Worker entry imports/runs under the current supported local Worker runtime;
- required node compatibility configuration is represented through the platform-supported artifact path;
- Drizzle migration starts an empty real local D1 with the same constraints and schema version;
- migration/bootstrap path is clean on a fresh store and refuses incompatible/nonempty/mismatched mapping;
- non-owner, forged/untrusted, wrong-origin and unset-ingress cases cannot initialize;
- concurrent first-owner attempts result in exactly one identical mapping;
- subsequent normal save/reopen/conflict/export/erasure behavior remains intact;
- production artifact contains no synthetic local identity path, private identity value or secret.

Do not weaken the existing requirement that `SITES_INGRESS_MODE=verified-private-sites` is an operator assertion enabled only after hosted ingress qualification. Local bootstrap tests establish application behavior, not real Sites header integrity.

## Repository and continuity handling

Re-observe canonical root `/Users/hynk/code/augnes`, origin, authenticated main, branch, HEAD and clean/dirty state. Read current AGENTS and relevant 01/02/03/04 owners, continuity/Companion owners and current verification policy.

Use a new issue-specific branch from then-current reviewed main; preserve unrelated work and all historical receipts.

B remains prepared with no native run/result. Do not fabricate a #1345 native completion. This compatibility repair is ordinary repository development justified by the hosted observation; continuity preparation is not a prerequisite unless current owners require it for explicit resume intent.

## Verification and stop

Use focused build/package/migration/D1/Browser checks during development. Exercise the **actual current Sites packaging contract locally** where available; a path-existence assertion alone is insufficient.

Because this changes executable build/distribution, migration and authorization/bootstrap behavior, follow the final exact-head planner and all required verification. Do not transfer #1346's receipt to changed source or rerun an unchanged failed deciding head merely for PASS.

Open one Draft PR linked to #1347 and #1345. Report:
- exact base/head/tree;
- actual current OpenAI Sites toolchain/version/commit evidence used;
- before/after artifact tree and packager result;
- migration source/output and local D1 evidence;
- mapping initialization mechanism and authority analysis;
- compatibility/security regressions;
- planner/final verification and cleanup;
- exact hosted handoff still pending.

Stop at review. No Site/D1 creation, version save, deployment, public access, real data, secret provisioning, existing-Site mutation, Ready, merge, or automatic hosted retry during this issue. After a reviewed merge, resume the already authorized hosted acceptance in #1345 using the same one-resource-pair limit.
